## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time non-rigid 3D reconstruction from monocular input, integrating differentiable rendering and on-device adaptation—critical for AR try-on. It also probes data strategy, evaluation, and edge-latency trade-offs in a commercial retail context.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular non-rigid 3D reconstruction with deformable garments
- Differentiable rendering and photometric consistency
- Temporal fusion for stability
- 5-shot on-device adaptation for unseen fabrics
- Edge latency and data strategy (synthetic+real)

## Code Example
```javascript
function forward(frame, params){
  const latent = encoder(frame, params.enc);
  const mesh = deformableMesh(latent, params.shape);
  const render = renderer(mesh, params.tex);
  const loss = photometric(render, frame) + temporal(mesh);
  return {mesh, loss};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle occlusion or loose fabrics?
- How evaluate mesh quality vs. latency?
- How would you construct synthetic data for textiles?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design end-to-end computer vision systems that fuse perception with physics for deformable objects in cluttered environments, while also demonstrating expertise in efficient on-device adaptation.

## Key Concepts
- On-device few-shot adaptation
- 2.5D depth and surface normals representation
- Differentiable physics for stiffness estimation
- Grasp affordance learning with temporal consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: training loop for temporally-consistent affordance learning
for (batch of data) {
  const pred = model.forward(images);
  const affordanceLoss = computeBCE(pred.heatmap, target.heatmap);
  const depthLoss = computeL1(pred.depth, target.depth);
  const physicsLoss = computePhysicsConsistency(pred.stiffness, pred.depth);

  const totalLoss = affordanceLoss + depthLoss + physicsLoss;
  optimizer.step(totalLoss);
}

